sion recalls the\n   marker. Print a clear PASS/FAIL line naming which of the two failed.\n\n## Gap 2 — long histories on opencode\n\nExtend `scripts/probe-large-history.mjs` so `opencode` is a supported target\nalongside `claude-code` and `codex`:\n\n    PROBE_TURNS=300 node scripts/probe-large-history.mjs opencode\n\nKeep the existing structure: it already takes target names as argv, seeds a\nhistory with a marker in the first turn and another in the last, resumes, and\nasks for both. The claude-code and codex paths must behave exactly as they do\ntoday — verify this by running them after your change.\n\nRequirements:\n\n1. Add the opencode resume invocation (headless, model pinned as above).\n2. The results table must report an accurate transcript size in KB for\n   opencode, the same as it does for the other two targets. Check what it\n   actually prints — a size of 0 means you have not finished this.\n3. Clean up after yourself completely. Confirm by listing opencode's sessions\n   before and after a run: the counts must match. A probe that leaves debris\n   in the user's session picker on every run is a defect.\n4. Do not raise the default `PROBE_TURNS`. 300 is the deliberate cap.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- `npm test` passes (currently 61 tests, all must still pass).\n- `npm run build` is clean.\n- Both probes actually **executed** against the real opencode binary, with\n  their real output pasted into the PR description. Do not open a PR\n  describing what the probes \"should\" do — the output is the deliverable.\n- `PROBE_TURNS=300 node scripts/probe-large-history.mjs claude-code codex`\n  still produces the same verdicts as before your change.\n- Session count in `opencode session list` is unchanged after running both\n  probes to completion.\n\n## Then update the docs\n\n- `docs/specs/opencode-session-format.md` §8: mark the two closed unknowns\n  RESOLVED in place, each with the date and the opencode version tested,\n  matching the style the claude/co