close the real source model in the import\nnotice text. `providerID` is a dispatch field, not a provenance field; putting\nprovenance there is what broke this. The governing rule (see the \"Fabrication\ncontract\" section of `docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md`, amended 2026-08-06) is that\nthe fabricated session has to *work* in the target, and honesty lives in the\ndisclosure.\n\n**Also match opencode's native message shape.** Native assistant messages wrap\ntheir text in `step-start` … `step-finish` parts; fabricated ones carry a bare\n`text` part. Emit the native scaffolding. Two reasons: the TUI is built around\nthose parts, and after the provider fix the recall run answered correctly and\nthen regenerated ~237 times (\"The loop has resumed …\") until it was killed —\nconsistent with a loop that never sees a terminal state, though a weak free\nmodel degenerating would look similar. Matching the native shape is required\nregardless of which it turns out to be; report which you observed once the parts\nare in place. Guard the probe with a timeout either way so it can never hang.\n\nGeneralize the lesson rather than special-casing opencode: fields like these\nbelong in tier 2 or 3 of the contract's three-tier model (portable / portable\nbut overridden per target / target-specific). If your fix adds an\nopencode-specific branch, make sure it lives behind the adapter boundary and is\nrecorded in the contract, not sprinkled through shared code.","oldString":"Your job is to fix this properly, which means resolving the tension rather than\njust hardcoding `\"opencode\"`: the provider/model must be resolvable by opencode\nso the session can be continued, while the source model's identity is still\npreserved somewhere honest (the import notice text is one option). Do not\nsilently claim the bridged turns were produced by a model that did not produce\nthem. State your chosen approach in the PR.\n\nOne more observation, **unconfirmed**, worth investigating while you are here:\nafter the provider fix, the recall run answered correctly and then kept\nregenerating — ~237 repetitions of the marker with filler like \"The loop has\nresumed\" — until the process was killed. That may be nothing more than a small\nfree model degenerating, but it may also be structural: fabricated assistant\nmessages carry only a `text` part, where native ones carry `step-start` …\n`step-finish` around it, so opencode's loop may never see a terminal state.\nDetermine which. If it is structural, fix it; if it is just the model, say so\nand move on. Guard the probe with a timeout either way so it cannot hang.","originalFile":"# Task: close the two opencode verification gaps in turnbridge\n\nRepo: `~/repos/evintunador/turnbridge` (branch off `main`).\n\nTurnbridge bridges conversations between coding-agent CLIs by fabricating\nnative session files/records that the target CLI's own resume loads. The\nopencode target adapter shipped 2026-08-02, but two verification gaps remain\nopen in `docs/specs/opencode-session-format.md` §8. Close both.\n\n## Read first\n\n- `scripts/probe-codex-content.mjs` — the model-recall probe for Codex. Your\n  new opencode probe is the analogue of this.\n- `scripts/probe-large-history.mjs` — the long-history probe. You will extend it.\n- `src/targets/opencode.ts` — the adapter. Note what `fabricate()` returns in\n  its `LaunchPlan`, especially `notes`.\n- `docs/specs/opencode-session-format.md` §8 — the gaps you are closing.\n\n## Gap 1 — headless model-recall probe, and the bug it exposes\n\nWrite `scripts/probe-opencode-content.mjs`.\n\nThe problem it solves: for opencode we have verified that fabricated history\n*renders* in the TUI, but never that the model *reads* it. An earlier attempt\nwas inconclusive because the configured provider errored on a **native**\nopencode session too, so the failure could not be attributed to fabrication.\n\n**That ambiguity is now resolved, and the news is bad.** Measured 2026-08-05\nagainst opencode 1.18.5 with a known-good provider:\n\n- A **native** opencode session with a planted marker resumes and recalls it\n  correctly via `opencode run -s <id> -m opencode/deepseek-v4-flash-free`.\n  Verified twice, before and after the failing runs below.\n- A **fabricated** session, same directory, same project, same model, minutes\n  apart, **never produces an assistant response at all.** `opencode run -s`\n  appends the user message to the session and exits **0 with completely empty\n  output** — no text, and no events even under `--format json`. Exporting the\n  session afterward shows the user turns accumulating with no assistant reply\n  between them.\n- Reproduced across three models (`deepseek-v4-flash-free`, `big-pickle`,\n  `ling-3.0-flash-free`), so it is structural, not model-specific.\n- When the session is filed under the **global** project (fabricating from a\n  directory opencode has no project for), the same continuation instead fails\n  loudly: `UnknownError / Unexpected server error`.\n\n**Root cause is diagnosed.** Running the failing resume with `--print-logs`\ngives:\n\n    ERROR message=failed error=\"ProviderModelNotFoundError:\n      Model not found: turnbridge/deepseek-v4-flash-free.\"\n\nThe adapter writes `providerID: \"turnbridge\"` on the session and on every\nfabricated message (`src/targets/opencode.ts` lines 321, 327, 388, 408).\nopencode resolves the model for its **agent loop** from the session's stored\nprovider/model, not from the `-m` flag or the TUI model picker — so it looks up\na provider literally named `turnbridge`, finds none, and exits the loop at\nstep 0 before issuing any request. The logs show `loop step=0` →\n`exiting loop` with no `stream` line, where a native session logs\n`process` → `stream` → `llm runtime selected`.\n\nThat provider id is deliberate: the fabrication contract says to propagate the\nsource model verbatim and never substitute a recognized-but-false one, and\n`docs/specs/opencode-session-format.md` §6 records the observed consequence as\na harmless one-time `Model turnbridge/<model> is not valid` toast, noting \"the\ncomposer falls back to the user's configured model ... History renders in full\neither way\". **That assessment is wrong and must be corrected**: the composer\ndisplay falls back, but the agent loop does not. The session is readable and\nun-continuable.\n\nVerified fix direction: editing only the four `\"providerID\": \"turnbridge\"`\noccurrences to `\"opencode\"` in an import payload, changing nothing else, makes\nthe model respond **and correctly recall a marker planted in the bridged\nhistory**. So fabrication itself is sound — the model does read the history.\n\nYour job is to fix this properly, which means resolving the tension rather than\njust hardcoding `\"opencode\"`: the provider/model must be resolvable by opencode\nso the session can be continued, while the source model's identity is still\npreserved somewhere honest (the import notice text is one option). Do not\nsilently claim the bridged turns were produced by a model that did not produce\nthem. State your chosen approach in the PR.\n\nOne more observation, **unconfirmed**, worth investigating while you are here:\nafter the provider fix, the recall run answered correctly and then kept\nregenerating — ~237 repetitions of the marker with filler like \"The loop has\nresumed\" — until the process was killed. That may be nothing more than a small\nfree model degenerating, but it may also be structural: fabricated assistant\nmessages carry only a `text` part, where native ones carry `step-start` …\n`step-finish` around it, so opencode's loop may never see a terminal state.\nDetermine which. If it is structural, fix it; if it is just the model, say so\nand move on. Guard the probe with a timeout either way so it cannot hang.\n\nSo this task has **two deliverables**: the probe, and a fix. A probe that\ncorrectly reports FAIL is not sufficient — the point is a fabricated opencode\nsession you can actually talk to.\n\nRequirements:\n\n1. Seed a two-turn conversation into a throwaway git repo via\n   `conversation-ledger`'s `appendEvents`, with a random secret marker in the\n   user turn. Fabricate an opencode session from it with\n   `targetFor(\"opencode\").fabricate(...)`.\n2. Resume headlessly and ask the model to reply with only the marker:\n   `opencode run -s <sessionId> -m opencode/deepseek-v4-flash-free \"<prompt>\"`.\n   Pin that model explicitly — do not rely on the machine's default model,\n   which is a local MLX server that is frequently down.\n3. **Run a native control first.** Create a normal opencode session with the\n   same model, plant a marker in it the same way a user would, resume it, and\n   ask for recall. If the control fails, the probe must report that the\n   provider is broken and exit distinctly — it must NOT report a fabrication\n   failure. This distinction is the entire reason the earlier attempt was\n   inconclusive; a probe that cannot make it is not worth having.\n   `opencode run --format json` emits the `sessionID` on every event, which is\n   a cleaner way to capture the control's id than diffing `session list`.\n4. Exit 0 only when the control passes AND the fabricated session recalls the\n   marker. Print a clear PASS/FAIL line naming which of the two failed.\n5. Note that the failure mode above is **exit 0 with empty output**. Any check\n   that trusts the exit code will report success on a session the model never\n   answered. Assert on recalled content, never on exit status alone.\n\n## Gap 2 — long histories on opencode\n\nExtend `scripts/probe-large-history.mjs` so `opencode` is a supported target\nalongside `claude-code` and `codex`:\n\n    PROBE_TURNS=300 node scripts/probe-large-history.mjs opencode\n\nKeep the existing structure: it already takes target names as argv, seeds a\nhistory with a marker in the first turn and another in the last, resumes, and\nasks for both. The claude-code and codex paths must behave exactly as they do\ntoday — verify this by running them after your change.\n\nRequirements:\n\n1. Add the opencode resume invocation (headless, model pinned as above).\n2. The results table must report an accurate transcript size in KB for\n   opencode, the same as it does for the other two targets. Check what it\n   actually prints — a size of 0 means you have not finished this.\n3. Clean up after yourself completely. Confirm by listing opencode's sessions\n   before and after a run: the counts must match. A probe that leaves debris\n   in the user's session picker on every run is a defect.\n4. Do not raise the default `PROBE_TURNS`. 300 is the deliberate cap.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- `npm test` passes (currently 88 tests, all must still pass).\n- `npm run build` is clean.\n- Both probes actually **executed** against the real opencode binary, with\n  their real output pasted into the PR description. Do not open a PR\n  describing what the probes \"should\" do — the output is the deliverable.\n- `PROBE_TURNS=300 node scripts/probe-large-history.mjs claude-code codex`\n  still produces the same verdicts as before your change.\n- Session count in `opencode session list` is unchanged after running both\n  probes to completion.\n- A fabricated opencode session, after your fix, answers a follow-up question\n  about its own history. Paste that exchange into the PR — it is the single\n  thing this task exists to produce.\n\n## Then update the docs\n\n- `docs/specs/opencode-session-format.md` §8: mark the two closed unknowns\n  RESOLVED in place, each with the date and the opencode version tested,\n  matching the style the claude/codex specs use for their resolved unknowns.\n  Record the measured numbers, not just \"works\".\n- `docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md`, the Roadmap section: the opencode bullet\n  currently ends with \"Remaining opencode gaps: no headless model-recall probe\n  ..., and long histories unmeasured\". Update it to reflect reality.\n- Add the new probe to the roadmap's list of revalidation tooling.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Node 20+, ESM, no new dependencies.\n- Match the surrounding code's style: these probes lead with a comment\n  explaining what question the probe answers and why that question is the\n  right one. Follow that.\n- You WILL need to modify `src/targets/opencode.ts` to fix the continuation\n  bug. Keep that change minimal and explain the root cause in the PR. If the\n  fix changes the import payload shape, update\n  `docs/specs/opencode-session-format.md` to match and add a regression test\n  next to the existing adapter tests.\n- Do not touch the version pins in `scripts/check-adapter-drift.mjs`.\n","replaceAll":false,"structuredPatch":[{"lines":[" the model respond **and correctly recall a marker planted in the bridged"," history**. So fabrication itself is sound — the model does read the history."," ","-Your job is to fix this properly, which means resolving the tension rather than","-just hardcoding `\"opencode\"`: the provider/model must be resolvable by opencode","-so the session can be continued, while the source model's identity is still","-preserved somewhere honest (the import notice text is one option). Do not","-silently claim the bridged turns were produced by a model that did not produce","-them. State your chosen approach in the PR.","+**The fix, decided — do not relitigate it.** Write a `providerID`/`modelID`","+opencode can actually resolve, and disclose the real source model in the import","+notice text. `providerID` is a dispatch field, not a provenance field; putting","+provenance there is what broke this. The governing rule (see the \"Fabrication","+contract\" section of `docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md`, amended 2026-08-06) is that","+the fabricated session has to *work* in the target, and honesty lives in the","+disclosure."," ","-One more observation, **unconfirmed**, worth investigating while you are here:","-after the provider fix, the recall run answered correctly and then kept","-regenerating — ~237 repetitions of the marker with filler like \"The loop has","-resumed\" — until the process was killed. That may be nothing more than a small","-free model degenerating, but it may also be structural: fabricated assistant","-messages carry only a `text` part, where native ones carry `step-start` …","-`step-finish` around it, so opencode's loop may never see a terminal state.","-Determine which. If it is structural, fix it; if it is just the model, say so","-and move on. Guard the probe with a timeout either way so it cannot hang.","+**Also match opencode's native message shape.** Native assistant messages wrap","+their text in `step-start` … `step-finish` parts; fabricated ones carry a bare","+`text` part. Emit the native scaffolding. Two reasons: the TUI is built around","+those parts, and after the provider fix the recall run answered correctly and","+then regenerated ~237 times (\"The loop has resumed …\") until it was killed —","+consistent with a loop that never sees a terminal state, though a weak free","+model degenerating would look similar. Matching the native shape is required","+regardless of which it turns out to be; report which you observed once the parts","+are in place. Guard the probe with a timeout either way so it can never hang."," ","+Generalize the lesson rather than special-casing opencode: fields like these","+belong in tier 2 or 3 of the contract's three-tier model (portable / portable","+but overridden per target / target-specific). If your fix adds an","+opencode-specific branch, make sure it lives behind the adapter boundary and is","+recorded in the contract, not sprinkled through shared code.","+"," So this task has **two deliverables**: the probe, and a fix. A probe that"," correctly reports FAIL is not sufficient — the point is a fabricated opencode"," session you can actually talk to."],"newLines":30,"newStart":72,"oldLines":23,"oldStart":72}],"userModified":false},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"39f